AU  - Guoqi Li
TI  - Integrating flexible service times and collection-and-delivery points into the first-last mile e-commerce distribution network design in mountainous rural areas
JO  - Research in Transportation Business & Management
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1016/j.rtbm.2026.101888
AB  - First-last mile e-commerce distribution in mountainous rural areas incurs higher costs and significant negative environmental impacts due to sparse populations and ecological fragility. While collection-and-delivery points (CDPs) and periodic service effectively address these issues, the literature seldom integrates the two and even less frequently considers service time flexibility, which may lead to limited operational flexibility and resource utilization. To bridge this gap, we explore the periodic location-routing problems with flexible service times and CDPs (PLRP-FC), and formulate a nonlinear mixed-integer programming model to minimize the total fixed costs of facilities and vehicles, as well as fuel consumption and carbon emissions costs. This model also incorporates critical real-world features, such as the impact of vehicle loads and road gradients on fuel consumption. Given the problem characteristics, we develop a problem-specific Benders decomposition (BD)-based heuristic algorithm, BD-APV, by integrating aggregation and partitioning preprocessing strategies with valid inequalities. A real-world case study in China demonstrates the model's and algorithm's effectiveness. BD-APV produces high-quality solutions within a reasonable timeframe, demonstrating that our proposed model can reduce total costs compared with fixed-schedule schemes, while preventing heavily loaded vehicles from climbing steep roads. Further analysis of key parameters provides stakeholders with actionable insights.
KW  - e-commerce distribution; mountainous rural areas; Benders decomposition; fuel consumption; CO2 emissions; vehicle loading; road slope; valid inequalities; mixed-integer nonlinear programming
UR  - https://openalex.org/W7214440381
